   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A shop sets the price of an item at \\( p(t) \\) dollars and sells \\( n(t) \\) items per day, where \\( t \\) is measured in weeks. At \\( t = 3 \\), \\( p(3) = 6 \\), \\( p'(3) = -1 \\), \\( n(3) = 13 \\), and \\( n'(3) = -2 \\). The daily revenue is \\( R(t) = p(t)n(t) \\) dollars per day. Find the value of \\( R'(3) \\), in dollars per day per week."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -25
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Record the values at \\( t = 3 \\): \\( p(3) = 6 \\), \\( p'(3) = -1 \\), \\( n(3) = 13 \\), \\( n'(3) = -2 \\).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "By the product rule, \\( (pn)'(3) = p'(3)n(3) + p(3)n'(3) \\).",
         "mathjson": 6
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Substitute: \\( (-1)(13) + (6)(-2) = -25 \\).",
         "mathjson": -25
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "So \\( R'(3) = -25 \\): revenue is changing at -25 dollars per day per week at \\( t = 3 \\).",
         "mathjson": -25
      }
   ],
